nodecode-godot-studio is a rewrite of Foolafroos's Foolafroos/godot-studio:

  https://github.com/Foolafroos/godot-studio
  commit 6c1d42d22ae6274f0ed4a3a9a264d2effe461736

MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Foolafroos (LICENSE). The original repository carries no
LICENSE file; plugin.yaml and the bundled skill's frontmatter declare MIT, so
LICENSE here is MIT's standard text under the repository's own holder.

What was rewritten: the plugin's four tools and the headless contract behind
them — finding a Godot 4 binary on PATH or at a desktop install's own path and
refusing in words when none is found, the headless check with --quit and
--quit-after N frames and its bounded tail of the engine's output, the export
from a named preset with --export-release and --export-debug, the resolution of
the output path (the preset's export_path when none is given) with the original's
two guards kept exactly: the output must resolve inside the project after
symlinks and .., and an existing file is only overwritten when it is a known
export artifact of that project; creating the output's parent directory; the
export-template hint; the export_presets.cfg reader that skips each preset's
.options section and answers every preset with the artifact's existence, size and
modification time; and the install report, narrowed to the engine and the
configured projects. The workflow guidance the plugin bundled as a skill is the
manual (help :godot-studio).

What was left out, and why: the desktop half (a status bar chip and a builds
gallery pane with its own read-only HTTP backend), because a Nodecode cell
answers through tools, /godot-studio and the manual rather than a separate window and
its port; and the godot-ai MCP registration in the install report (a `mcp list`
call and `uv` detection, which name another harness's command), because the MCP
server belongs to a different product and this cell's report is about this one.
